Python syntax definition (https://invent.kde.org/frameworks/syntax-highlighting)

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Provenance

`python.xml` is a derivative of the KDE Kate/KWrite highlighting module for
Python, whose own header credits, in order, Per Wigren (v0.9), Michael Bueker
(v1.9), Paul Giannaros (v1.97 and v2.01) and Primoz Anzur (v1.99), with later
revisions up to v2.29 unattributed in the file.

It is vendored from the copy that skylighting embeds, which is what Pandoc uses:
repository `jgm/skylighting`, path `skylighting-core/xml/python.xml`, commit
`fcaf8658` (2026-07-26, the last commit to touch that file). That copy is
byte-identical to KDE `frameworks/syntax-highlighting` at
`data/syntax/python.xml` on `master`, verified 2026-11-07 at 136,838 bytes on
both sides.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Licence: not declared upstream

The upstream file declares no licence, and this is a fact about it rather than
an omission here. Three measurements, all taken on 2026-11-07:

  - the `<language>` element carries `license=""`, the file carries no SPDX
    header, there is no `python.xml.license` sibling in the KDE repository, and
    that repository publishes no REUSE metadata covering `data/syntax/`
  - KDE's `README.md` states that contributions to KSyntaxHighlighting "shall be
    licensed under MIT", a policy this file's authorship chain predates
  - skylighting's own `README.md` states only that "some" of the bundled KDE XML
    files are LGPL- or GPL-licensed, which is why the `skylighting` package ships
    under the GPL while `skylighting-core`, which carries the XML, is BSD3

hebstr-doc therefore redistributes this file without asserting a licence for it,
under whichever of the above applies, and claims no licence of its own over it.
It is aggregated with, not part of, the MIT-licensed remainder of hebstr-doc: no
other file in this extension derives from it.

Unlike `syntax/r.xml` (GPL v2) and `template.dotx` (GPL v2 or later), this file
is not a copyleft component of the extension, because no copyleft terms are
declared for it. Should upstream add an SPDX header, this section is what has to
be rewritten, and the third-party table of `LICENSE.md` with it.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Changes

Twelve changes were made, each marked `hebstr` in the file on every line it
touches. Every one of them moves a token onto a default style whose colour in
`theme-base.scss` is already the value the maintainer's Positron configuration
resolves for the matching TextMate scope, so none of them introduces a colour:

  - the `funcdefs` list and the `Function Definition` item (`dsImport`): `def`
    leaves the `defs` list and stops sharing a style with `class`, `del`,
    `global`, `lambda` and `nonlocal`
  - the `Import` item moves from `dsImport` to `dsKeyword`, the counterpart of
    the change above, `import`, `from` and `as` being `keyword.control`
  - the `langconstants` list and the `Language Constant` item (`dsConstant`):
    `None`, `True`, `False`, `NotImplemented` and `Ellipsis` leave
    `specialvars` and stop sharing a style with `self` and the dunders
  - the `Function Name` item (`dsFunction`) and the two rules that reach it, a
    `RegExpr` on the identifier in front of `(` and the `DefName` context the
    `def` keyword enters. Upstream tokenises neither the defined name nor a
    non-builtin call
  - the `Caps Constant` item (`dsRegionMarker`) and its `RegExpr`: an ALL_CAPS
    name, which upstream leaves as unstyled normal text
  - the `Decorator` item moves from `dsAttribute` to `dsFunction`
  - the operator `AnyChar` of the `Normal` context gains `,` and `.`, both
    untokenised upstream

Unlike `r.xml`, the language is **not** renamed and no Lua filter is needed:
Quarto ships no Python definition of its own, so there is no last-wins collision
to dodge. The file name still is: skylighting derives the
`class="sourceCode <name>"` it emits from the basename, so a file named anything
other than `python.xml` produces markup no stylesheet targets.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

Refresh procedure

    gh api "repos/jgm/skylighting/contents/skylighting-core/xml/python.xml?ref=<commit>" \
      --jq '.content' | base64 -d > python-upstream.xml

Re-apply the twelve changes above, update the commit recorded here, and run
`bash tests/python-syntax-tokens.sh`, which asserts each of them in a real
render. Four things the re-application has to preserve, in that order of
fragility:

  1. the two identifier rules stay **ahead of** `DetectIdentifier` in the
     `Normal` context: that rule consumes every bare identifier, so a rule
     appended after it never fires
  2. the two split lists stay split, upstream adding items to both over time
  3. the two characters added to the operator `AnyChar` stay **after**
     `IncludeRules context="Number"`, so a float keeps its own token
  4. the six `itemData` mappings
